== Evidence Run Narrative ==
Run: 2026-03-12_evidence-v1.5
Goal: Head-to-head comparison of four experiment-selection approaches on real train.py.

--- 10:49 — Run started ---
Four approaches will run sequentially on the same hardware (GPU 1, dllm-experiment.home),
same schema (DEPTH × MATRIX_LR × WEIGHT_DECAY, 27 cells), same budget (20 experiments each),
same seed (42). Each experiment trains a GPT model for ~5 minutes and measures val_bpb.

Approaches:
  1. random     — uniform random cell selection (control)
  2. bayesian   — Thompson sampling + two-step lookahead, no LLM
  3. autoresearch — LLM-only with flat results log (Karpathy-style)
  4. full       — Bayesian model + LLM with appraisal context (autoresearcher2)

--- 12:27 — Silent fallback bug discovered ---
Critical instrumentation flaw found: when the LLM fails in the autoresearch approach,
the code falls back to random selection but labels it as "llm_flat" in the data.
This would make the comparison meaningless — we couldn't tell if the LLM actually drove
the experiment selection or if random chance happened to look like LLM performance.

Fix committed (ce7d19a): each decision now carries a truthful source label.
New labels distinguish llm_flat, llm_fallback_error, llm_fallback_empty, and random_init.

Decision: let random + bayesian finish (they don't use LLM, so their data is clean).
Kill the process before autoresearch starts. Rerun autoresearch + full with fixed code.

--- 12:38 — Random complete ---
20/20 experiments. Best val_bpb = 1.033551 (DEPTH=8, MATRIX_LR=0.04, WEIGHT_DECAY=0.4).
Mean = 1.054828. Runtime: 109 minutes.

Early patterns: DEPTH=8 cells consistently outperform DEPTH=10. WEIGHT_DECAY=0.4 dominates.
Throughput varies 3x across depths: ~1M tok/s (DEPTH=6) vs ~300K tok/s (DEPTH=10).
Reproducibility is strong: cell 11 was sampled twice, Δval_bpb = 0.000006.

--- 14:27 — Bayesian complete, process killed ---
20/20 experiments. Best val_bpb = 1.033778 (DEPTH=8, MATRIX_LR=0.04, WEIGHT_DECAY=0.2).
Mean = 1.051689. Runtime: 108.5 minutes.

The Bayesian model's convergence is clearly visible in the surprise trajectory:
  - Experiments 1-5: surprise 0.07 → 0.04 (rapid learning, exploring DEPTH=10 first)
  - Experiments 5-10: surprise 0.04 → 0.004 (switching to DEPTH=8, refining)
  - Experiments 10-20: surprise < 0.001 (model converged, filling coverage gaps)

The model effectively ranked all 27 cells from ~15 experiments. Epistemic score dropped
from 6.0 to 0.034 — almost no uncertainty remaining.

Watcher script detected the autoresearch start marker and killed PID 45609 at 14:27:11.
Data verified: random + bayesian both 20/20, zero errors, zero fallbacks.

--- 14:31 — Autoresearch restarted with fixed code ---
New process (PID 67756), new run_id (65018a7c). Fixed source labeling confirmed working:
first experiment correctly labeled random_init, subsequent ones llm_flat.

--- 15:37 — Autoresearch halfway, LLM performing well ---
11/20 experiments saved. Best val_bpb = 1.033266 (DEPTH=8, MATRIX_LR=0.04, WEIGHT_DECAY=0.2).
This is already better than both random (1.033551) and bayesian (1.033778) after their full 20.

Source labels: 1× random_init, 10× llm_flat. Zero fallbacks. The fixed instrumentation
confirms every selection was genuinely made by the LLM.

LLM behavior:
  - Experiments 2-3: started with DEPTH=10 (poor choice), learned quickly from bad results
  - From experiment 4 onward: almost exclusively DEPTH=8 — found the sweet spot fast
  - Experiment 8 (best): DEPTH=8, MATRIX_LR=0.04, WEIGHT_DECAY=0.2 — notably a different
    WEIGHT_DECAY than random/bayesian's best (0.4). May be finding a subtler optimum.
  - Mean val_bpb (1.045) already lower than random (1.055) and bayesian (1.052),
    suggesting the LLM wastes fewer experiments on poor regions of the space.

No decisions taken — run proceeding autonomously. Full approach starts after autoresearch.

--- 16:21 — Autoresearch complete ---
20/20 experiments (19 successful, 1 with LLM fallback at experiment 20).
Best val_bpb = 1.032934 (DEPTH=8, MATRIX_LR=0.04, WEIGHT_DECAY=0.2, experiment 17).
Mean val_bpb = 1.047409.

Source integrity: 1× random_init, 16× llm_flat, 3× llm_fallback_empty.
The 3 fallbacks occurred in the last 5 experiments when the LLM ran out of novel suggestions
in the small 27-cell space. All fallbacks are honestly labeled.

Key observation: autoresearch found the best val_bpb of any approach so far (1.032934 vs
random's 1.033551 and bayesian's 1.033778). It also had the lowest mean, suggesting the
LLM wastes fewer experiments on poor regions.

--- 16:41 — Full approach started (first attempt) ---
Started with original code (no enriched prompt). Killed at experiment 1 to restart
with improved LLM prompt that includes:
  - Token counts per experiment (exposing the throughput confound)
  - Prior approach results (random, bayesian, autoresearch)
  - Epistemic governance context from CONSTITUTION and CHARTER
  - Explicit explanation of the token budget confound

GPU memory issue: previous train.py process (PID 3708643) still held 38GB GPU memory.
Killed remote process, verified GPU clear.

--- 16:46 — Full approach restarted with enriched prompt ---
New process (PID 39828), new run_id (d240b43f). First 2 experiments failed (GPU OOM
from stale process), then recovered. The enriched prompt is working: LLM reasoning
now references prior approach results and the token budget confound.

--- 17:32 — Full approach halfway ---
11/20 experiments (8 successful after 2 initial OOM failures).
Best val_bpb = 1.033423. Sources: mix of lookahead and llm_augmented.

The LLM's reasoning quality has visibly improved with the enriched context:
  - References "prior approaches consistently found DEPTH=8/MATRIX_LR=0.04"
  - Considers "the token budget confound favoring DEPTH=6"
  - Proposes testing untested corners "across all approaches"
  - Shows genuine cross-approach learning instead of isolated exploration
